/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 11-15
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:11, name:"The Broken Covenant", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 11:4",verse:"Which I commanded your fathers in the day that I brought them forth out of the land of Egypt, from the iron furnace, saying, Obey my voice, and do them, according to all which I command you: so shall ye be my people, and I will be your God:"}, facts:["Did you know God calls Egypt 'the iron furnace' in this chapter, a picture of the fierce heat of Israel's bondage (Jeremiah 11:4)?","Did you know Judah had as many gods as it had cities, and altars to Baal stood on the streets of Jerusalem (Jeremiah 11:13)?","Did you know Jeremiah's own neighbors in Anathoth plotted to kill him and demanded he stop prophesying in the Lord's name (Jeremiah 11:21)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who was Jeremiah told to speak the covenant words to?", options:["The men of Judah, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem","The priests of Shiloh","The elders of Samaria","The captives in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:2",verse:"Hear ye the words of this covenant, and speak unto the men of Judah, and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem;", insight:"God names the hearers plainly; His word comes to specific people, not to a vague crowd."},
{q:"What did the LORD say about the man who obeys not the covenant?", options:["Cursed be the man that obeyeth not the words of this covenant","Blessed be the man who only hears them","He shall be sent into exile at once","He shall pay a fine to the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:3",verse:"And say thou unto them, Thus saith the LORD God of Israel; Cursed be the man that obeyeth not the words of this covenant,", insight:"Disobedience carries a curse, not a shrug; God takes His covenant words seriously."},
{q:"What name did God give to Egypt in the covenant words?", options:["The iron furnace","The house of shadows","The land of plenty","The bitter river"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:4",verse:"Which I commanded your fathers in the day that I brought them forth out of the land of Egypt, from the iron furnace, saying, Obey my voice, and do them, according to all which I command you: so shall ye be my people, and I will be your God:", insight:"Egypt is remembered as an iron furnace; God delivered them from fire, not from mild inconvenience."},
{q:"What promise came with obeying God's voice?", options:["So shall ye be my people, and I will be your God","Ye shall conquer all nations","Ye shall never know sorrow","Ye shall possess Egypt's gold"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:4",verse:"Which I commanded your fathers in the day that I brought them forth out of the land of Egypt, from the iron furnace, saying, Obey my voice, and do them, according to all which I command you: so shall ye be my people, and I will be your God:", insight:"The covenant's heart is belonging; obedience opens the way to 'my people' and 'your God.'"},
{q:"How did Jeremiah answer God's oath?", options:["So be it, O LORD","It is too hard, O LORD","Send another, O LORD","Not yet, O LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:5",verse:"That I may perform the oath which I have sworn unto your fathers, to give them a land flowing with milk and honey, as it is this day. Then answered I, and said, So be it, O LORD.", insight:"'So be it' is the prophet's yes to God's plan; agreement comes before understanding."},
{q:"Where was Jeremiah to proclaim the covenant words?", options:["In the cities of Judah, and in the streets of Jerusalem","In the temple courts only","At the borders of Egypt","In the wilderness of Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:6",verse:"Then the LORD said unto me, Proclaim all these words in the cities of Judah, and in the streets of Jerusalem, saying, Hear ye the words of this covenant, and do them.", insight:"The word belonged in the streets, not sealed in a scroll; truth is meant to be proclaimed aloud."},
{q:"How had God pleaded with the fathers?", options:["Earnestly protested, rising early and protesting","Sent them away without warning","Punished them at once","Ignored their disobedience"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:7",verse:"For I earnestly protested unto your fathers in the day that I brought them up out of the land of Egypt, even unto this day, rising early and protesting, saying, Obey my voice.", insight:"God rose early to plead with them; His patience worked the morning shift long before judgment came."},
{q:"What did the fathers do instead of obeying?", options:["Walked every one in the imagination of their evil heart","Fled to Egypt for help","Served the LORD faithfully","Repented in sackcloth"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:8",verse:"Yet they obeyed not, nor inclined their ear, but walked every one in the imagination of their evil heart: therefore I will bring upon them all the words of this covenant, which I commanded them to do; but they did them not.", insight:"Each one followed his own imagined way; the evil heart needs no company to go wrong."},
{q:"What did the LORD say was found among Judah and Jerusalem?", options:["A conspiracy","A hidden treasure","A new temple","A secret treaty"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:9",verse:"And the LORD said unto me, A conspiracy is found among the men of Judah, and among the inhabitants of Jerusalem.", insight:"Conspiracy among God's own people; rebellion against Him is never a private matter."},
{q:"What had the house of Israel and the house of Judah broken?", options:["The covenant God made with their fathers","The walls of Jerusalem","The treaty with Egypt","The vessels of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:10",verse:"They are turned back to the iniquities of their forefathers, which refused to hear my words; and they went after other gods to serve them: the house of Israel and the house of Judah have broken my covenant which I made with their fathers.", insight:"They turned back to their forefathers' iniquities; breaking covenant repeats old sins."},
{q:"What evil would God bring upon them?", options:["Evil which they shall not be able to escape","A mild rebuke","Another warning only","A second chance at once"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:11",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD, Behold, I will bring evil upon them, which they shall not be able to escape; and though they shall cry unto me, I will not hearken unto them.", insight:"Evil they could not escape; grace refused becomes judgment endured."},
{q:"To whom would they cry in the time of their trouble?", options:["To the gods unto whom they offer incense","To the king of Babylon","To the prophets of the LORD","To the armies of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:12",verse:"Then shall the cities of Judah and inhabitants of Jerusalem go, and cry unto the gods unto whom they offer incense: but they shall not save them at all in the time of their trouble.", insight:"They would cry to the gods they had served; idols are loud in comfort and silent in trouble."},
{q:"How many gods did Judah have?", options:["According to the number of thy cities","Only one true God","Seven chief gods","As many as the stars"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:13",verse:"For according to the number of thy cities were thy gods, O Judah; and according to the number of the streets of Jerusalem have ye set up altars to that shameful thing, even altars to burn incense unto Baal.", insight:"A god for every city; when gods multiply, so does confusion."},
{q:"What stood on the streets of Jerusalem?", options:["Altars to burn incense unto Baal","Gardens for the priests","Markets of the merchants","Statues of the kings"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:13",verse:"For according to the number of thy cities were thy gods, O Judah; and according to the number of the streets of Jerusalem have ye set up altars to that shameful thing, even altars to burn incense unto Baal.", insight:"Altars to Baal lined the streets; shame had become public decoration."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah forbidden to do for this people?", options:["Pray not for them, nor lift up a cry or prayer","Preach to them no more","Write their names in a book","Leave the city of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:14",verse:"Therefore pray not thou for this people, neither lift up a cry or prayer for them: for I will not hear them in the time that they cry unto me for their trouble.", insight:"Pray not for this people; there comes a point when intercession is refused, not rewarded."},
{q:"What did God ask of His beloved?", options:["What hath my beloved to do in mine house?","Why hast thou forsaken me?","Where are thy sacrifices?","Who taught thee to pray?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:15",verse:"What hath my beloved to do in mine house, seeing she hath wrought lewdness with many, and the holy flesh is passed from thee? when thou doest evil, then thou rejoicest.", insight:"'What hath my beloved to do in mine house?' Belovedness does not license lewdness."},
{q:"What did the LORD call Judah's name?", options:["A green olive tree, fair, and of goodly fruit","A cedar of Lebanon","A fruitful vine","A strong tower"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:16",verse:"The LORD called thy name, A green olive tree, fair, and of goodly fruit: with the noise of a great tumult he hath kindled fire upon it, and the branches of it are broken.", insight:"A green olive tree, fair and fruitful; God names what He planted with honor, even in judgment."},
{q:"Who had planted the tree that God would burn?", options:["The LORD of hosts, that planted thee","The kings of Judah","The priests of the temple","The nations round about"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:17",verse:"For the LORD of hosts, that planted thee, hath pronounced evil against thee, for the evil of the house of Israel and of the house of Judah, which they have done against themselves to provoke me to anger in offering incense unto Baal.", insight:"The LORD of hosts planted them; the hand that plants also prunes."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah like among those who plotted against him?", options:["Like a lamb or an ox brought to the slaughter","Like a lion among sheep","Like a thief in the night","Like a soldier in battle"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:19",verse:"But I was like a lamb or an ox that is brought to the slaughter; and I knew not that they had devised devices against me, saying, Let us destroy the tree with the fruit thereof, and let us cut him off from the land of the living, that his name may be no more remembered.", insight:"He knew not they devised devices against him; innocence is often the last to suspect betrayal."},
{q:"What did they plot to do to Jeremiah?", options:["Cut him off from the land of the living, that his name be remembered no more","Send him to Egypt in chains","Strip him of his priesthood","Force him to serve Baal"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 11:19",verse:"But I was like a lamb or an ox that is brought to the slaughter; and I knew not that they had devised devices against me, saying, Let us destroy the tree with the fruit thereof, and let us cut him off from the land of the living, that his name may be no more remembered.", insight:"They wanted his name forgotten; God remembered it in Scripture instead."}
]},
{n:12, name:"Why Do the Wicked Prosper?", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 12:5",verse:"If thou hast run with the footmen, and they have wearied thee, then how canst thou contend with horses? and if in the land of peace, wherein thou trustedst, they wearied thee, then how wilt thou do in the swelling of Jordan?"}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah's question in this chapter, 'Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper?', is one of Scripture's boldest complaints to God (Jeremiah 12:1)?","Did you know the 'swelling of Jordan' in verse 5 refers to the lush, flood-prone thickets of the Jordan River, a dangerous place to run (Jeremiah 12:5)?","Did you know God promised that even Judah's evil neighbors would be restored if they learned to swear by His name, 'The LORD liveth' (Jeremiah 12:16)?"], questions:[
{q:"How did Jeremiah open his complaint to God?", options:["Righteous art thou, O LORD, when I plead with thee","I am innocent, O LORD, hear my cause","Judge me quickly, O LORD","Hide thy face from me, O LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:1",verse:"Righteous art thou, O LORD, when I plead with thee: yet let me talk with thee of thy judgments: Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper? wherefore are all they happy that deal very treacherously?", insight:"He calls God righteous even while questioning; faith argues, but never accuses."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's great question?", options:["Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper?","Why was I born into this generation?","When will the exile end?","Who will lead the people?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:1",verse:"Righteous art thou, O LORD, when I plead with thee: yet let me talk with thee of thy judgments: Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper? wherefore are all they happy that deal very treacherously?", insight:"'Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper?' An honest question that God is big enough to receive."},
{q:"Where was God in the lives of the wicked?", options:["Near in their mouth, and far from their reins","Near in their hearts","Far from their lips","Absent from their thoughts"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:2",verse:"Thou hast planted them, yea, they have taken root: they grow, yea, they bring forth fruit: thou art near in their mouth, and far from their reins.", insight:"Near in their mouth, far from their reins; God-talk without God-obedience is hollow."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah ask God to do with the wicked?", options:["Pull them out like sheep for the slaughter","Forgive them at once","Send them to Egypt","Hide them in the hills"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:3",verse:"But thou, O LORD, knowest me: thou hast seen me, and tried mine heart toward thee: pull them out like sheep for the slaughter, and prepare them for the day of slaughter.", insight:"Pull them out like sheep for the slaughter; Jeremiah handed the sentence to God instead of taking it himself."},
{q:"Why did the land mourn?", options:["For the wickedness of them that dwell therein","Until the rains return","For forty days","Until the king repents"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:4",verse:"How long shall the land mourn, and the herbs of every field wither, for the wickedness of them that dwell therein? the beasts are consumed, and the birds; because they said, He shall not see our last end.", insight:"The land mourned for the wickedness of its dwellers; sin poisons more than souls."},
{q:"What was God's first answer to Jeremiah?", options:["If thou hast run with the footmen and they have wearied thee, how canst thou contend with horses?","Be still and wait","Flee to the mountains","Speak no more of this"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:5",verse:"If thou hast run with the footmen, and they have wearied thee, then how canst thou contend with horses? and if in the land of peace, wherein thou trustedst, they wearied thee, then how wilt thou do in the swelling of Jordan?", insight:"If footmen weary you, how will you run with horses? God was preparing him for harder ground."},
{q:"What was God's second challenge?", options:["How wilt thou do in the swelling of Jordan?","How wilt thou cross the sea?","How wilt thou climb the hills?","How wilt thou face the king?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:5",verse:"If thou hast run with the footmen, and they have wearied thee, then how canst thou contend with horses? and if in the land of peace, wherein thou trustedst, they wearied thee, then how wilt thou do in the swelling of Jordan?", insight:"The swelling of Jordan was worse than the land of peace; God trains His servants for the flood, not just the field."},
{q:"Who had dealt treacherously with Jeremiah?", options:["His brethren, and the house of his father","The king and his court","The priests of Jerusalem","The men of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:6",verse:"For even thy brethren, and the house of thy father, even they have dealt treacherously with thee; yea, they have called a multitude after thee: believe them not, though they speak fair words unto thee.", insight:"His own brethren dealt treacherously; betrayal hurts most when it comes from home."},
{q:"What had God done with His house and heritage?", options:["Forsaken His house, left His heritage, given the beloved of His soul into enemies' hands","Burned the temple at once","Sold the people to Egypt","Hidden the ark forever"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:7",verse:"I have forsaken mine house, I have left mine heritage; I have given the dearly beloved of my soul into the hand of her enemies.", insight:"God gave the dearly beloved of His soul into enemies' hands; judgment began at His own house."},
{q:"What was God's heritage like to Him?", options:["As a lion in the forest, crying out against Him","As a dove in the cote","As a lamb in the fold","As an eagle in the sky"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:8",verse:"Mine heritage is unto me as a lion in the forest; it crieth out against me: therefore have I hated it.", insight:"His heritage roared against Him like a forest lion; God felt the rebellion personally."},
{q:"What bird image did God use for His heritage?", options:["A speckled bird, with the birds round about against her","A caged dove","A night owl","A swift swallow"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:9",verse:"Mine heritage is unto me as a speckled bird, the birds round about are against her; come ye, assemble all the beasts of the field, come to devour.", insight:"A speckled bird that the other birds attack; Israel stood out, and the nations circled."},
{q:"Who destroyed God's vineyard?", options:["Many pastors","The Babylonian soldiers","The locusts of the field","The kings of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:10",verse:"Many pastors have destroyed my vineyard, they have trodden my portion under foot, they have made my pleasant portion a desolate wilderness.", insight:"Many pastors destroyed the vineyard; leaders answer for what they trample."},
{q:"Why was the whole land made desolate?", options:["Because no man layeth it to heart","Because the rains failed","Because the king fled","Because the walls fell"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:11",verse:"They have made it desolate, and being desolate it mourneth unto me; the whole land is made desolate, because no man layeth it to heart.", insight:"The land mourned because no man laid it to heart; indifference is its own kind of desolation."},
{q:"Who came upon all the high places?", options:["The spoilers; the sword of the LORD shall devour from one end of the land to the other","The shepherds with their flocks","The merchants with their goods","The pilgrims to the feast"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:12",verse:"The spoilers are come upon all high places through the wilderness: for the sword of the LORD shall devour from the one end of the land even to the other end of the land: no flesh shall have peace.", insight:"The spoilers came on the high places; no corner of the land escaped the sword."},
{q:"What did they sow, and what would they reap?", options:["Sown wheat, but shall reap thorns","Sown barley, but shall reap tares","Sown nothing, and shall reap nothing","Sown tears, and shall reap joy"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:13",verse:"They have sown wheat, but shall reap thorns: they have put themselves to pain, but shall not profit: and they shall be ashamed of your revenues because of the fierce anger of the LORD.", insight:"They sowed wheat and would reap thorns; the harvest of sin never matches the seed."},
{q:"What would they be ashamed of?", options:["Their revenues, because of the fierce anger of the LORD","Their poverty","Their king","Their prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:13",verse:"They have sown wheat, but shall reap thorns: they have put themselves to pain, but shall not profit: and they shall be ashamed of your revenues because of the fierce anger of the LORD.", insight:"Ashamed of their revenues; profit that provokes God's anger is a shameful wage."},
{q:"What would God do to His evil neighbors?", options:["Pluck them out of their land, and pluck the house of Judah from among them","Burn their cities at once","Send them to Egypt","Spare them for Judah's sake"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:14",verse:"Thus saith the LORD against all mine evil neighbours, that touch the inheritance which I have caused my people Israel to inherit; Behold, I will pluck them out of their land, and pluck out the house of Judah from among them.", insight:"God would pluck out the evil neighbors; those who touch His inheritance answer to Him."},
{q:"What would God do after plucking them out?", options:["Return, have compassion, and bring every man to his heritage and land","Forget them forever","Make them slaves","Scatter them further"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:15",verse:"And it shall come to pass, after that I have plucked them out I will return, and have compassion on them, and will bring them again, every man to his heritage, and every man to his land.", insight:"After plucking, compassion; God's judgment leaves the door open to restoration."},
{q:"What would build the neighbors in the midst of God's people?", options:["Diligently learning to swear by His name, The LORD liveth","Paying tribute to Jerusalem","Building a new temple","Serving the king of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:16",verse:"And it shall come to pass, if they will diligently learn the ways of my people, to swear by my name, The LORD liveth; as they taught my people to swear by Baal; then shall they be built in the midst of my people.", insight:"Learning to swear by His name; even strangers could be built in, if they honored the LORD."},
{q:"What would happen to the nation that would not obey?", options:["He would utterly pluck up and destroy that nation","He would warn them again","He would leave them alone","He would send more prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 12:17",verse:"But if they will not obey, I will utterly pluck up and destroy that nation, saith the LORD.", insight:"Utterly plucked up and destroyed; mercy offered and refused becomes judgment."}
]},
{n:13, name:"The Marred Girdle", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 13:11",verse:"For as the girdle cleaveth to the loins of a man, so have I caused to cleave unto me the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah, saith the LORD; that they might be unto me for a people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory: but they would not hear."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah wore a new linen girdle to the Euphrates and hid it in a rock, only to dig up a ruined rag after many days (Jeremiah 13:4-7)?","Did you know God said He would fill every bottle with wine as a parable, meaning He would fill kings, priests, prophets, and all Jerusalem with drunkenness (Jeremiah 13:12-13)?","Did you know this chapter gives one of Scripture's most quoted sayings about stubborn sin: 'Can the Ethiopian change his skin, or the leopard his spots?' (Jeremiah 13:23)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Jeremiah told to get?", options:["A linen girdle","A woolen cloak","A leather belt","A silver chain"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD unto me, Go and get thee a linen girdle, and put it upon thy loins, and put it not in water.", insight:"A linen girdle, fresh and clean; the sign began with something made for closeness and honor."},
{q:"What was he forbidden to do with the girdle?", options:["Put it not in water","Wear it in the temple","Show it to the king","Take it to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD unto me, Go and get thee a linen girdle, and put it upon thy loins, and put it not in water.", insight:"Put it not in water; keep the sign new, so the ruin would speak louder."},
{q:"Where was he to hide the girdle?", options:["At Euphrates, in a hole of the rock","In the Jordan's banks","Under the temple floor","In a cave at Tekoa"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:4",verse:"Take the girdle that thou hast got, which is upon thy loins, and arise, go to Euphrates, and hide it there in a hole of the rock.", insight:"Hidden by the Euphrates; the river of coming exile was the place of the lesson."},
{q:"What was the girdle like when he dug it up?", options:["Marred, profitable for nothing","Washed clean and white","Stolen by travelers","Turned to dust"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:7",verse:"Then I went to Euphrates, and digged, and took the girdle from the place where I had hid it: and, behold, the girdle was marred, it was profitable for nothing.", insight:"Marred and profitable for nothing; neglect in a damp hiding place ruins what was made for honor."},
{q:"What did God say He would mar?", options:["The pride of Judah, and the great pride of Jerusalem","The walls of the temple","The armies of Babylon","The ships of Tarshish"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:9",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, After this manner will I mar the pride of Judah, and the great pride of Jerusalem.", insight:"God would mar their pride; what exalts itself against Him gets ruined."},
{q:"How did God describe this people?", options:["Evil people, which refuse to hear my words","A holy nation","A faithful remnant","A quiet people"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:10",verse:"This evil people, which refuse to hear my words, which walk in the imagination of their heart, and walk after other gods, to serve them, and to worship them, shall even be as this girdle, which is good for nothing.", insight:"Evil people who refuse to hear; refusal is the root the rest grew from."},
{q:"What would they become like?", options:["As the girdle, which is good for nothing","As gold tried in fire","As a fruitful tree","As a strong wall"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:10",verse:"This evil people, which refuse to hear my words, which walk in the imagination of their heart, and walk after other gods, to serve them, and to worship them, shall even be as this girdle, which is good for nothing.", insight:"Good for nothing, like the ruined girdle; sin turns useful things into waste."},
{q:"Why had God caused Israel and Judah to cleave to Him?", options:["That they might be unto me for a people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory","That they might pay tribute","That they might fight His battles","That they might build His temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:11",verse:"For as the girdle cleaveth to the loins of a man, so have I caused to cleave unto me the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah, saith the LORD; that they might be unto me for a people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory: but they would not hear.", insight:"For a people, a name, a praise, a glory; closeness to God was meant to show Him off."},
{q:"What went wrong with God's purpose?", options:["They would not hear","They were too few","They lived too far","They had no priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:11",verse:"For as the girdle cleaveth to the loins of a man, so have I caused to cleave unto me the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah, saith the LORD; that they might be unto me for a people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory: but they would not hear.", insight:"They would not hear; the girdle's purpose failed at a closed ear."},
{q:"Who would be filled with drunkenness?", options:["Kings on David's throne, priests, prophets, and all Jerusalem","Only the foreign armies","The merchants of Tyre","The shepherds of the fields"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:13",verse:"Then shalt thou say unto them, Thus saith the LORD, Behold, I will fill all the inhabitants of this land, even the kings that sit upon David’s throne, and the priests, and the prophets, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, with drunkenness.", insight:"Kings, priests, prophets, all Jerusalem; judgment would start at the top and fill the whole land."},
{q:"What would God do to them?", options:["Dash them one against another, even fathers and sons together","Send them to Egypt","Hide them in the hills","Teach them a new song"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:14",verse:"And I will dash them one against another, even the fathers and the sons together, saith the LORD: I will not pity, nor spare, nor have mercy, but destroy them.", insight:"Dashed one against another, fathers and sons; division at home is judgment's cruelest work."},
{q:"What plea opens the warning?", options:["Hear ye, and give ear; be not proud","Flee to the mountains","Hide in the caves","Pay your vows"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:15",verse:"Hear ye, and give ear; be not proud: for the LORD hath spoken.", insight:"'Be not proud'; pride is the sin this whole chapter keeps circling."},
{q:"What were they to do before darkness came?", options:["Give glory to the LORD your God","Offer more sacrifices","Build higher walls","Seek help from Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:16",verse:"Give glory to the LORD your God, before he cause darkness, and before your feet stumble upon the dark mountains, and, while ye look for light, he turn it into the shadow of death, and make it gross darkness.", insight:"Give glory before He cause darkness; honor God while you can still see your way."},
{q:"What would Jeremiah's soul do if they would not hear?", options:["Weep in secret places for their pride","Rejoice in their fall","Flee to the wilderness","Curse them aloud"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:17",verse:"But if ye will not hear it, my soul shall weep in secret places for your pride; and mine eye shall weep sore, and run down with tears, because the LORD’S flock is carried away captive.", insight:"His soul would weep in secret for their pride; the true prophet grieves over the judgment he announces."},
{q:"What were the king and the queen told to do?", options:["Humble yourselves, sit down","Flee to Babylon","Gather the armies","Close the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:18",verse:"Say unto the king and to the queen, Humble yourselves, sit down: for your principalities shall come down, even the crown of your glory.", insight:"Humble yourselves, sit down; crowns come off before God brings them down."},
{q:"What would happen to Judah?", options:["Carried away captive all of it, wholly carried away captive","Spared for the king's sake","Left in peace","Made rulers of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:19",verse:"The cities of the south shall be shut up, and none shall open them: Judah shall be carried away captive all of it, it shall be wholly carried away captive.", insight:"Wholly carried away captive; the word 'wholly' leaves no loophole."},
{q:"From where would the attackers come?", options:["From the north","From the south","From the east","From the sea"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:20",verse:"Lift up your eyes, and behold them that come from the north: where is the flock that was given thee, thy beautiful flock?", insight:"From the north they would come; God had named the direction long before the army marched."},
{q:"What question did God ask about change?", options:["Can the Ethiopian change his skin, or the leopard his spots?","Can the lion change its roar?","Can the sea change its waves?","Can the sun change its rising?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:23",verse:"Can the Ethiopian change his skin, or the leopard his spots? then may ye also do good, that are accustomed to do evil.", insight:"The Ethiopian cannot change his skin; settled sin feels fixed, and the verse leaves the cure to God alone."},
{q:"What would God do with them?", options:["Scatter them as the stubble that passeth away by the wind","Gather them as wheat","Plant them as trees","Hide them as treasure"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:24",verse:"Therefore will I scatter them as the stubble that passeth away by the wind of the wilderness.", insight:"Scattered as stubble by the wilderness wind; pride blows away like chaff."},
{q:"What was Jerusalem's final cry in the chapter?", options:["Woe unto thee, O Jerusalem! wilt thou not be made clean?","Peace unto thee, O Jerusalem!","Sleep well, O Jerusalem!","Arise, O Jerusalem!"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 13:27",verse:"I have seen thine adulteries, and thy neighings, the lewdness of thy whoredom, and thine abominations on the hills in the fields. Woe unto thee, O Jerusalem! wilt thou not be made clean? when shall it once be?", insight:"'Wilt thou not be made clean?' Even at the end, the question is an invitation."}
]},
{n:14, name:"The Dearth", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 14:22",verse:"Are there any among the vanities of the Gentiles that can cause rain? or can the heavens give showers? art not thou he, O LORD our God? therefore we will wait upon thee: for thou hast made all these things."}, facts:["Did you know this whole chapter was given 'concerning the dearth,' a devastating drought when Judah's gates languished and the cry of Jerusalem went up (Jeremiah 14:1-2)?","Did you know the nobles sent their little ones to the pits for water, and the children came home with empty vessels, ashamed and covering their heads (Jeremiah 14:3)?","Did you know false prophets were promising 'assured peace' while God said they prophesied lies and would themselves be consumed by sword and famine (Jeremiah 14:13-15)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was this word of the LORD concerning?", options:["The dearth","The harvest","The temple","The king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:1",verse:"The word of the LORD that came to Jeremiah concerning the dearth.", insight:"The word came concerning the dearth; God speaks into droughts, not just about them."},
{q:"How did Judah look in the drought?", options:["Mourneth, with gates languishing, black unto the ground","Green and flourishing","Busy with harvest","Full of singing"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:2",verse:"Judah mourneth, and the gates thereof languish; they are black unto the ground; and the cry of Jerusalem is gone up.", insight:"Judah mourned, black unto the ground; the land wore the people's grief."},
{q:"What did the nobles' little ones find at the pits?", options:["No water; they returned with vessels empty","Bitter water","Muddy water","Plenty of water"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:3",verse:"And their nobles have sent their little ones to the waters: they came to the pits, and found no water; they returned with their vessels empty; they were ashamed and confounded, and covered their heads.", insight:"Empty vessels came home; the pits of earth cannot hold what heaven withholds."},
{q:"Why were the plowmen ashamed?", options:["The ground was chapt, for there was no rain","The seed was stolen","The oxen died","The king took their land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:4",verse:"Because the ground is chapt, for there was no rain in the earth, the plowmen were ashamed, they covered their heads.", insight:"The ground was chapt for lack of rain; the plowman cannot argue with a closed heaven."},
{q:"What did the hind do in the field?", options:["Calved, and forsook it, because there was no grass","Fled to the mountains","Drank from the river","Hid her young"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:5",verse:"Yea, the hind also calved in the field, and forsook it, because there was no grass.", insight:"The hind forsook her newborn for lack of grass; drought breaks even a mother's instinct."},
{q:"What did the wild asses do?", options:["Stood in the high places, snuffing up the wind like dragons","Dug for water","Attacked the villages","Migrated south"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:6",verse:"And the wild asses did stand in the high places, they snuffed up the wind like dragons; their eyes did fail, because there was no grass.", insight:"They snuffed the wind like dragons, eyes failing; when grass fails, even the wild grow desperate."},
{q:"What did the people plead?", options:["Though our iniquities testify against us, do thou it for thy name's sake","We are innocent","Punish our enemies","Send us a new king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:7",verse:"O LORD, though our iniquities testify against us, do thou it for thy name’s sake: for our backslidings are many; we have sinned against thee.", insight:"They pled God's name, not their record; when iniquities testify against you, appeal to His name."},
{q:"Who is the hope of Israel?", options:["The LORD, the saviour thereof in time of trouble","The king of Judah","The armies of Egypt","The prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:8",verse:"O the hope of Israel, the saviour thereof in time of trouble, why shouldest thou be as a stranger in the land, and as a wayfaring man that turneth aside to tarry for a night?", insight:"'O the hope of Israel'; trouble is the time a Saviour's name shines brightest."},
{q:"What did they ask about God's presence?", options:["Why shouldest thou be as a stranger in the land?","Why hast thou left us forever?","Where is thy temple?","Who shall find thee?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:9",verse:"Why shouldest thou be as a man astonied, as a mighty man that cannot save? yet thou, O LORD, art in the midst of us, and we are called by thy name; leave us not.", insight:"Why be as a stranger in the land? The question of every believer in a dark hour."},
{q:"What had this people loved?", options:["To wander; they have not refrained their feet","To rest at home","To serve the LORD","To keep the feasts"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:10",verse:"Thus saith the LORD unto this people, Thus have they loved to wander, they have not refrained their feet, therefore the LORD doth not accept them; he will now remember their iniquity, and visit their sins.", insight:"They loved to wander; feet that will not be refrained walk into judgment."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah told about praying for them?", options:["Pray not for this people for their good","Pray day and night","Fast for forty days","Weep for the children"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:11",verse:"Then said the LORD unto me, Pray not for this people for their good.", insight:"Pray not for this people; God closed the door He usually holds open."},
{q:"How would God consume them?", options:["By the sword, and by the famine, and by the pestilence","By fire from heaven","By the beasts of the field","By the flood waters"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:12",verse:"When they fast, I will not hear their cry; and when they offer burnt offering and an oblation, I will not accept them: but I will consume them by the sword, and by the famine, and by the pestilence.", insight:"Sword, famine, pestilence; fasting and offerings could not buy off what sin had ordered."},
{q:"What did the false prophets promise?", options:["Ye shall not see the sword, neither famine; assured peace","War with Babylon","A new temple","Rain within days"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:13",verse:"Then said I, Ah, Lord GOD! behold, the prophets say unto them, Ye shall not see the sword, neither shall ye have famine; but I will give you assured peace in this place.", insight:"'Assured peace' from lying lips; the smoothest promises can be the deadliest."},
{q:"What did God say of those prophets?", options:["They prophesy lies in my name: I sent them not","They are my true servants","They speak too boldly","They must be honored"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:14",verse:"Then the LORD said unto me, The prophets prophesy lies in my name: I sent them not, neither have I commanded them, neither spake unto them: they prophesy unto you a false vision and divination, and a thing of nought, and the deceit of their heart.", insight:"I sent them not; a prophet without a sending is a voice without authority."},
{q:"What would happen to the lying prophets?", options:["By sword and famine shall those prophets be consumed","They would be exiled","They would be forgiven","They would be made priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:15",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD concerning the prophets that prophesy in my name, and I sent them not, yet they say, Sword and famine shall not be in this land; By sword and famine shall those prophets be consumed.", insight:"The very sword and famine they denied would consume them; lies about judgment do not stop it."},
{q:"What would happen to the people who believed them?", options:["Cast out in the streets, with none to bury them","Spared for their ignorance","Sent to Egypt","Given new prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:16",verse:"And the people to whom they prophesy shall be cast out in the streets of Jerusalem because of the famine and the sword; and they shall have none to bury them, them, their wives, nor their sons, nor their daughters: for I will pour their wickedness upon them.", insight:"Cast out in the streets unburied; believing a lie does not excuse the ruin it brings."},
{q:"What tears was Jeremiah to shed?", options:["Eyes running down with tears night and day","Tears of joy","No tears at all","Tears only in secret"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:17",verse:"Therefore thou shalt say this word unto them; Let mine eyes run down with tears night and day, and let them not cease: for the virgin daughter of my people is broken with a great breach, with a very grievous blow.", insight:"Tears night and day, without ceasing; the weeping prophet earned his name here."},
{q:"What did the people confess?", options:["We acknowledge our wickedness, and the iniquity of our fathers","We are righteous","Our enemies are worse","We know nothing"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:20",verse:"We acknowledge, O LORD, our wickedness, and the iniquity of our fathers: for we have sinned against thee.", insight:"They owned their wickedness and their fathers'; confession that names sin is the first step home."},
{q:"What did they ask for God's name's sake?", options:["Do not abhor us, do not disgrace the throne of thy glory","Give us gold","Send us rain at once","Destroy Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:21",verse:"Do not abhor us, for thy name’s sake, do not disgrace the throne of thy glory: remember, break not thy covenant with us.", insight:"Do not disgrace the throne of Thy glory; they pled God's reputation, His covenant with them."},
{q:"Who can cause rain?", options:["None among the vanities of the Gentiles; the heavens cannot give showers","The priests of Baal","The king's magicians","The clouds themselves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 14:22",verse:"Are there any among the vanities of the Gentiles that can cause rain? or can the heavens give showers? art not thou he, O LORD our God? therefore we will wait upon thee: for thou hast made all these things.", insight:"No idol can cause rain; only the LORD who made all these things holds the clouds."}
]},
{n:15, name:"A Fenced Brazen Wall", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 15:20",verse:"And I will make thee unto this people a fenced brasen wall: and they shall fight against thee, but they shall not prevail against thee: for I am with thee to save thee and to deliver thee, saith the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know God said that even Moses and Samuel, the two great intercessors, standing before Him could not turn His mind toward this people (Jeremiah 15:1)?","Did you know God appointed 'four kinds' of judgment here: the sword to slay, dogs to tear, the fowls of heaven, and the beasts of the earth to devour and destroy (Jeremiah 15:3)?","Did you know this chapter gives the beloved line 'Thy words were found, and I did eat them,' Jeremiah's picture of devouring Scripture with joy (Jeremiah 15:16)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who could not turn God's mind toward this people?", options:["Though Moses and Samuel stood before me","Though David and Solomon prayed","Though the priests offered","Though the king repented"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:1",verse:"Then said the LORD unto me, Though Moses and Samuel stood before me, yet my mind could not be toward this people: cast them out of my sight, and let them go forth.", insight:"Even Moses and Samuel could not turn Him; some judgments outrun even the greatest intercessors."},
{q:"What four fates did God name?", options:["Death, the sword, the famine, the captivity","War, plague, fire, flood","Exile, slavery, hunger, thirst","Shame, pain, loss, grief"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:2",verse:"And it shall come to pass, if they say unto thee, Whither shall we go forth? then thou shalt tell them, Thus saith the LORD; Such as are for death, to death; and such as are for the sword, to the sword; and such as are for the famine, to the famine; and such as are for the captivity, to the captivity.", insight:"Death, sword, famine, captivity; each would find its own appointed portion."},
{q:"What four kinds did God appoint over them?", options:["The sword to slay, dogs to tear, fowls of heaven, beasts of earth","Lions, bears, wolves, serpents","Hail, fire, darkness, locusts","Thieves, murderers, liars, traitors"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:3",verse:"And I will appoint over them four kinds, saith the LORD: the sword to slay, and the dogs to tear, and the fowls of the heaven, and the beasts of the earth, to devour and destroy.", insight:"Sword, dogs, fowls, beasts; creation itself was conscripted for judgment."},
{q:"Why would they be removed into all kingdoms of the earth?", options:["Because of Manasseh son of Hezekiah, for what he did in Jerusalem","Because of the sins of Ahab","Because of the golden calf","Because of the tower of Babel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:4",verse:"And I will cause them to be removed into all kingdoms of the earth, because of Manasseh the son of Hezekiah king of Judah, for that which he did in Jerusalem.", insight:"Because of Manasseh; one king's wickedness can poison generations."},
{q:"Who would pity Jerusalem?", options:["Who shall have pity upon thee? Who shall go aside to ask how thou doest?","All the nations","The king of Babylon","Her own children"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:5",verse:"For who shall have pity upon thee, O Jerusalem? or who shall bemoan thee? or who shall go aside to ask how thou doest?", insight:"Who would turn aside to ask how she did? Forsaken Jerusalem found no comforters."},
{q:"What had the people done?", options:["Forsaken me, gone backward","Forgotten the Sabbath","Stolen from the temple","Worshipped in secret"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:6",verse:"Thou hast forsaken me, saith the LORD, thou art gone backward: therefore will I stretch out my hand against thee, and destroy thee; I am weary with repenting.", insight:"Forsaken and gone backward; God is weary with repenting toward those who keep retreating."},
{q:"What had increased above the sand of the seas?", options:["Their widows","Their armies","Their ships","Their debts"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:8",verse:"Their widows are increased to me above the sand of the seas: I have brought upon them against the mother of the young men a spoiler at noonday: I have caused him to fall upon it suddenly, and terrors upon the city.", insight:"Widows more than the sand of the seas; war's arithmetic is counted in empty homes."},
{q:"What happened to her that had borne seven?", options:["She languisheth, gave up the ghost, her sun gone down while yet day","She fled to Egypt","She became queen","She was healed"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:9",verse:"She that hath borne seven languisheth: she hath given up the ghost; her sun is gone down while it was yet day: she hath been ashamed and confounded: and the residue of them will I deliver to the sword before their enemies, saith the LORD.", insight:"Her sun went down while it was yet day; a full house emptied before noon."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah to the whole earth?", options:["A man of strife and a man of contention","A man of peace","A silent watcher","A wealthy merchant"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:10",verse:"Woe is me, my mother, that thou hast borne me a man of strife and a man of contention to the whole earth! I have neither lent on usury, nor men have lent to me on usury; yet every one of them doth curse me.", insight:"A man of strife to the whole earth; faithfulness can feel like a fight with everyone."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah say about usury?", options:["I have neither lent on usury, nor men have lent to me; yet every one doth curse me","I lent to many","I borrowed from kings","I forgave all debts"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:10",verse:"Woe is me, my mother, that thou hast borne me a man of strife and a man of contention to the whole earth! I have neither lent on usury, nor men have lent to me on usury; yet every one of them doth curse me.", insight:"He had wronged no one in business, yet all cursed him; hatred of the message becomes hatred of the messenger."},
{q:"What did God promise Jeremiah?", options:["Verily it shall be well with thy remnant","Thou shalt die in peace","Thy enemies shall prosper","Thou shalt be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:11",verse:"The LORD said, Verily it shall be well with thy remnant; verily I will cause the enemy to entreat thee well in the time of evil and in the time of affliction.", insight:"It shall be well with thy remnant; God keeps a promise inside the judgment."},
{q:"What would happen to their substance and treasures?", options:["Given to the spoil without price, for all thy sins","Stored in the temple","Sent to Egypt","Buried in the fields"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:13",verse:"Thy substance and thy treasures will I give to the spoil without price, and that for all thy sins, even in all thy borders.", insight:"Given to the spoil without price; sin sells cheap what God gave rich."},
{q:"Where would they pass with their enemies?", options:["Into a land which thou knowest not","Into Egypt","Into Assyria","Into the desert"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:14",verse:"And I will make thee to pass with thine enemies into a land which thou knowest not: for a fire is kindled in mine anger, which shall burn upon you.", insight:"Into a land they knew not; exile is punishment with a strange address."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah do with God's words?", options:["Found them, and did eat them","Hid them in a jar","Burned them","Sold them"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:16",verse:"Thy words were found, and I did eat them; and thy word was unto me the joy and rejoicing of mine heart: for I am called by thy name, O LORD God of hosts.", insight:"He ate the words, and they became joy; Scripture digested turns into delight."},
{q:"Why did Jeremiah sit alone?", options:["I sat not in the assembly of the mockers; I sat alone because of thy hand","He was ill","He was in prison","He was fasting"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:17",verse:"I sat not in the assembly of the mockers, nor rejoiced; I sat alone because of thy hand: for thou hast filled me with indignation.", insight:"He sat alone because of God's hand; holiness can be a lonely seat."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's complaint?", options:["Why is my pain perpetual, and my wound incurable?","Why am I poor?","Why do I age?","Why am I hated?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:18",verse:"Why is my pain perpetual, and my wound incurable, which refuseth to be healed? wilt thou be altogether unto me as a liar, and as waters that fail?", insight:"Why is my pain perpetual? Even prophets ask God the hard 'why.'"},
{q:"What did he fear God might be to him?", options:["As a liar, and as waters that fail","As a stranger","As an enemy","As a judge"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:18",verse:"Why is my pain perpetual, and my wound incurable, which refuseth to be healed? wilt thou be altogether unto me as a liar, and as waters that fail?", insight:"As waters that fail in summer; he feared God's help would run dry like a brook."},
{q:"What did God promise if Jeremiah returned?", options:["I will bring thee again, and thou shalt stand before me","Thou shalt be king","Thou shalt be rich","Thou shalt rest"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:19",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD, If thou return, then will I bring thee again, and thou shalt stand before me: and if thou take forth the precious from the vile, thou shalt be as my mouth: let them return unto thee; but return not thou unto them.", insight:"If thou return, I will bring thee again; God restores the servant who turns back."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah to take forth?", options:["The precious from the vile, and be as God's mouth","Hide them both","Sell the vile","Keep the precious"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:19",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD, If thou return, then will I bring thee again, and thou shalt stand before me: and if thou take forth the precious from the vile, thou shalt be as my mouth: let them return unto thee; but return not thou unto them.", insight:"Take the precious from the vile; discernment is the prophet's daily work."},
{q:"What would God make Jeremiah to the people?", options:["A fenced brasen wall","A broken reed","A swift horse","A tall tower"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 15:20",verse:"And I will make thee unto this people a fenced brasen wall: and they shall fight against thee, but they shall not prevail against thee: for I am with thee to save thee and to deliver thee, saith the LORD.", insight:"A fenced brazen wall; God makes His servant unbreakable, not unattacked."}
]}
]);
